WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.960
Welcome everyone to this year's course on Mobile Sensing and Robotics.

00:05.960 --> 00:10.840
My name is Serge Stachness and I'm a professor here at the Photogrammetry and Robotics Lab

00:10.840 --> 00:12.620
at the University of Bonn.

00:12.620 --> 00:18.500
It's my pleasure to welcome you and give you a very short overview of what we're going

00:18.500 --> 00:22.460
to do in this course and some of the formal requirements.

00:22.460 --> 00:26.880
So this is a course, first of all, which is taught jointly between the English Master

00:26.880 --> 00:31.920
Program Geodetic Engineering and the German Master Program Geodesy and Geo-Information.

00:31.920 --> 00:37.200
So your whole number either starts with MSR or with M, depending on which study program

00:37.200 --> 00:41.600
you're in, but it's content-wise the same course over here.

00:41.600 --> 00:47.120
And we are looking into mobile systems that move through the environment, perceive the

00:47.120 --> 00:52.680
environment, model the environment, but also take actions in order to reach a certain goal,

00:52.680 --> 00:58.440
for example, in order to navigate through a certain goal location or in order to cover

00:58.440 --> 01:00.840
an environment with its sensors.

01:00.840 --> 01:05.560
So we're looking into basic techniques that mobile robots and other autonomous devices

01:05.560 --> 01:10.480
use in order to perceive, understand and act in their world.

01:10.480 --> 01:14.440
And that's what the main topic of this course is about.

01:14.440 --> 01:19.820
We are looking here really into the foundations, how to do basic state estimation, recursive

01:19.820 --> 01:25.620
filtering, localization, basic control problems, planning.

01:25.620 --> 01:28.780
All these topics are part of that course.

01:28.780 --> 01:35.120
So when I talk about autonomous mobile systems or robots, then we can mean devices like those

01:35.120 --> 01:36.120
over here.

01:36.120 --> 01:41.880
This can be autonomous, small-scale vehicles equipped with sensors that move through the

01:41.880 --> 01:42.880
environment.

01:42.880 --> 01:50.480
This can be UAVs flying around observing objects of the Earth.

01:50.480 --> 01:56.520
This could in theory also be satellites which perceive data and that data that we need to

01:56.520 --> 02:02.760
interpret, something that often, at least in the space systems, doesn't run online for

02:02.760 --> 02:10.240
the observations of the Earth, but the analysis of the data is typically done offline, but

02:10.240 --> 02:14.080
that's not really a difference in terms of the state estimation algorithm that you could

02:14.080 --> 02:17.580
use for interpreting certain types of data.

02:17.580 --> 02:23.680
We can look into systems, planetary rovers, for example, there everything happens on board.

02:23.680 --> 02:27.360
Those systems need to perceive what happens around them, especially what the world looks

02:27.360 --> 02:30.740
like and how to navigate in this world.

02:30.740 --> 02:36.320
And also autonomous cars, our systems can be seen as robots that move through the environment,

02:36.320 --> 02:40.560
mobile sensing platforms that need to achieve a certain goal, for example, is reaching a

02:40.560 --> 02:42.760
certain target location.

02:42.760 --> 02:49.280
And if you look into the fundamental problems or fundamental questions that we can raise

02:49.280 --> 02:53.280
in mobile robotics, then these are those two questions here.

02:53.280 --> 02:55.880
First, what is the state of the world?

02:55.880 --> 03:00.520
And the second one, which action should I execute in order to reach a certain goal state,

03:00.520 --> 03:03.560
a desired goal state, a place where I want to be?

03:04.000 --> 03:09.080
OK, so the first question takes sensor data into account and turns the sensor data into

03:09.080 --> 03:11.460
some form of useful information.

03:11.460 --> 03:15.200
So there are typically some state variables that the system is interested in knowing.

03:15.200 --> 03:16.960
This could be its own location.

03:16.960 --> 03:23.160
This could be the location of 3D points in the environment or any other type of information

03:23.160 --> 03:26.960
that the system can gather through its sensor data.

03:26.960 --> 03:29.520
And this is typically a state estimation problem.

03:29.880 --> 03:35.140
Once we know the state of the world or we have a probability distribution describing

03:35.140 --> 03:42.560
us how that state is probably looking like, then we can decide what to do in order to

03:42.560 --> 03:45.000
bring the world into a different state.

03:45.000 --> 03:48.960
Bringing the world into a different state could mean that the system executes a navigation

03:48.960 --> 03:51.640
command to move itself to a different place.

03:51.640 --> 03:55.040
But this could also mean that we are changing things in the environment, that we are moving

03:55.040 --> 03:56.320
objects around.

03:56.320 --> 04:01.240
So which action to execute in order to become closer to a desired state.

04:01.240 --> 04:03.640
And these are two common problems.

04:03.640 --> 04:08.320
This is a state estimation problem and this is an action selection problem.

04:08.320 --> 04:13.540
And the state estimation is something that most of you have seen already in their past.

04:13.540 --> 04:18.520
These squares problems in order to generate a fit between what you're actually observing

04:18.520 --> 04:22.360
and what you're expected to observe given your state configuration is a form of state

04:22.360 --> 04:25.640
estimation that's an offline state estimation problem.

04:25.640 --> 04:28.920
In this course here we look a little bit more into online state estimation problems,

04:28.920 --> 04:33.380
typically filtering techniques such as a Kalman filter or the particle filter, where we want

04:33.380 --> 04:38.780
to look into a way for doing this state estimation in a recursive fashion.

04:38.780 --> 04:43.420
And given we have a belief about the state of the world, then we can decide what should

04:43.420 --> 04:47.740
we do in order to bring the current state closer to a desired state.

04:47.740 --> 04:49.240
And this is an action selection problem.

04:49.240 --> 04:51.840
We can use planning techniques in here.

04:51.960 --> 04:57.220
Decision processes, for example, are a different way for especially dealing with uncertainties

04:57.220 --> 04:58.800
in the action execution.

04:58.800 --> 05:03.480
These are techniques that we will invest or investigate in this course, study in this

05:03.480 --> 05:09.060
course, in order to get a deeper understanding how those mobile systems can actually work.

05:09.060 --> 05:11.880
So in terms of perception, that's what we are going to start with.

05:11.880 --> 05:15.180
There are two kind of key perception problems.

05:15.180 --> 05:19.760
The first one is related to estimating semantic information, assessing semantic information.

05:19.760 --> 05:23.300
And the second one is related to estimating geometric information.

05:23.300 --> 05:26.880
So semantic information means, for example, we take that image and want to interpret which

05:26.880 --> 05:32.120
part of the image belongs to a certain semantic class, so like being a crop or a wheat plant

05:32.120 --> 05:33.120
in this example.

05:33.120 --> 05:36.680
And this is something that we are not really covering in this course this time.

05:36.680 --> 05:42.420
This is something that we will investigate more in the upcoming terms than when we look

05:42.420 --> 05:45.400
into learning problems.

05:45.400 --> 05:49.060
Because this typically involves machine learning techniques, which is not the main focus of

05:49.060 --> 05:51.700
this course here today.

05:51.700 --> 05:56.900
We are looking more into estimating geometry, so trying to estimate where are 3D points

05:56.900 --> 05:59.480
in the environment, where is an object in that world.

05:59.480 --> 06:01.420
That's what we're going to start with.

06:01.420 --> 06:04.820
Okay, let's dive a little bit deeper and look into the individual problem that we're looking

06:04.820 --> 06:05.820
into.

06:05.820 --> 06:10.820
So first of all, in this course, there's a short kind of primer on Python because Python

06:10.820 --> 06:13.900
is a programming language that we will use in this course.

06:13.900 --> 06:17.580
So we kind of partially excluded it from this course in the sense that it doesn't show up

06:17.580 --> 06:23.380
here on the lecture slides, but there is a short collection of videos giving you introduction

06:23.380 --> 06:26.460
into Python in case this hasn't happened yet.

06:26.460 --> 06:29.500
And this is the basic for the homework assignments, for example.

06:29.500 --> 06:33.700
In the lectures itself, Python will not play a large role, so we're not looking into code

06:33.700 --> 06:39.260
itself here in the lecture, but it's a key and essential part for the homework assignments.

06:39.260 --> 06:43.700
We will start here with, again, a short repetition of a probability primer, basic concepts of

06:43.700 --> 06:49.340
probability theory, which will then allow us to derive the base filter, which is a general

06:49.340 --> 06:52.380
framework for recursive state estimation.

06:52.380 --> 06:54.980
We are then looking into occupancy grid maps.

06:54.980 --> 07:00.380
This is the question, is a part of the environment occupied by an obstacle, yes or no?

07:00.380 --> 07:04.860
So basically building maps which tell us something about the occupancy of individual parts of

07:04.860 --> 07:05.860
the world.

07:05.860 --> 07:09.900
And this is a basic representation that is used, for example, for path planning.

07:09.900 --> 07:14.580
You can envision them similar to architectural floor plans, for example, where the walls

07:14.580 --> 07:18.860
are drawn and certain key structures of the building, and then there's white space, which

07:18.860 --> 07:20.500
is basically your free space.

07:20.500 --> 07:24.700
And occupancy grid maps are actually very similar to those representations, except that

07:24.700 --> 07:30.140
they are built from sensor data and take the current state of the environment into account.

07:30.140 --> 07:33.760
Based on those maps, we then can perform localization tasks.

07:33.760 --> 07:37.260
Localization can be done with Kalman filters, can be done with particle filters, and that's

07:37.260 --> 07:40.060
something that we're going to look into.

07:40.060 --> 07:44.980
In order to localize a platform, we first need to understand how they move through the

07:44.980 --> 07:45.980
environment.

07:45.980 --> 07:50.320
So basic principles of locomotion, so kind of what kind of command do I need to give

07:50.320 --> 07:53.860
to a motor in order to make the system move.

07:53.860 --> 07:57.580
So motion and motion models, probabilistic motion models will play an important role

07:57.580 --> 08:01.560
here, but also the interpretation of the sensor data, kind of what does it mean if I get a

08:01.560 --> 08:05.020
sensor measurement, what is the uncertainty associated with this measurement, at least

08:05.020 --> 08:09.580
in its very basic form will be investigated, because then that's the basic for actually

08:09.580 --> 08:14.220
implementing a base filter, a recursive state estimation technique, because for that we

08:14.220 --> 08:20.260
typically need to have models about what is the result of an action and what does an observation

08:20.260 --> 08:21.840
mean in there.

08:21.840 --> 08:25.900
And again, this will allow us to solve basic state estimation problem, and then in the

08:25.900 --> 08:30.100
second part of the course we are looking into the active parts, looking into robot control.

08:30.100 --> 08:36.140
So in this year especially we extended the control part a little bit with providing a

08:36.140 --> 08:40.860
bit of a short introduction also into MPC, and then we look into motion planning here,

08:40.860 --> 08:44.700
looking into basic planning algorithms such as ASTAR, but also looking into a mark of

08:44.700 --> 08:50.020
decision processes in order to have an idea on how we can deal at least with basic uncertainties

08:50.020 --> 08:52.040
in this process.

08:52.040 --> 08:57.080
And so this course gives you an introduction into basic concepts of mobile robots and systems

08:57.080 --> 09:00.640
that move through the environment in order to perform state estimation and will be the

09:00.640 --> 09:06.160
basis for most other activities that are happening here in the lab.

09:06.160 --> 09:10.760
If you look into any robotic system that is out there today, let it be an autonomous car,

09:10.760 --> 09:16.020
let it be your vacuum cleaner, they will use elements of what we teach here in this course,

09:16.020 --> 09:21.020
how to move through the environment, how to estimate their own state, how to perceive

09:21.020 --> 09:22.020
the world.

09:22.020 --> 09:25.840
These are aspects that we are going to cover in this course, and the basic principles you

09:25.840 --> 09:31.660
find also in much more advanced techniques today, or systems today, although their techniques

09:31.660 --> 09:34.700
may be more advanced, more adapted to their application.

09:34.700 --> 09:38.840
But we are starting here with the basics to give you the foundations to understand what

09:38.840 --> 09:41.340
builds on top of this.

09:41.340 --> 09:45.500
So my name is Sore Stachnus, I'm here responsible for this course and will take care about the

09:45.500 --> 09:51.100
majority of the lectures, how you can reach me either in person, Nusale 15 is where I'm

09:51.100 --> 09:55.280
located, where my office is, you can send me an email which is the easiest way to reach

09:55.280 --> 10:01.500
me, or you can call me also on the phone in the office if there is an urgent need to do

10:01.500 --> 10:02.500
that.

10:02.500 --> 10:06.740
Besides myself, there will be student assistants helping you with your homework assignments,

10:06.740 --> 10:11.300
doing the tutorial hopefully in person this year.

10:11.300 --> 10:16.120
If in person doesn't work out, then we have to turn this into Zoom, but this depends on

10:16.120 --> 10:19.420
the current situation that we are facing this year.

10:19.420 --> 10:22.040
Let's see how that evolves.

10:22.040 --> 10:27.020
Before I want to end this introduction, I want to briefly look into some formal aspects

10:27.020 --> 10:31.660
that are important for this course if you enrolled here in this course at Bonn University

10:31.660 --> 10:36.260
and I'm taking it as a student on the internet, so to say.

10:36.260 --> 10:42.100
So homework assignments are given out and they come out with a deadline attached to

10:42.100 --> 10:43.100
it.

10:43.100 --> 10:45.000
And it's important to kind of stick to this deadline.

10:45.000 --> 10:50.980
So on eCampus, which is a system which we're using here in Bonn in order to share the homework

10:50.980 --> 10:54.680
assignments and we have to also submit the results of your homework assignments, you

10:54.680 --> 10:58.520
will see your homework assignments and on this homework assignment is a deadline and

